{{- /*
  Windows Terminal "fragment extension" — a native, documented mechanism
  (https://learn.microsoft.com/windows/terminal/json-fragment-extensions) for third parties to
  contribute profiles/color schemes WITHOUT touching the user's real settings.json. Works
  regardless of whether Windows Terminal is packaged (winget/Store) or not, since this path is
  the generic third-party drop location, not Windows Terminal's own config file.

  UPDATED 2026-08-19 (user's explicit call): modifies the user's actual default Command Prompt
  and PowerShell profiles directly via `"updates"` (a fragment CAN target the built-in default
  profiles and dynamic profiles this way — Microsoft's own docs confirm "You can modify only
  the default profiles, Command Prompt and PowerShell, as well as dynamic profiles through
  fragments") instead of adding a separate "Dotfiles" profile nobody would actually use by
  default (Windows Terminal fragments still can't set defaultProfile — that's the one thing
  only settings.json itself can do, deliberately left alone, see CONCEPT_ROADMAP.md §7).

  GUIDs below are Windows Terminal's own fixed identifiers for these profiles, confirmed
  directly from its source (not just cited elsewhere) 2026-08-19:
    - Command Prompt: defaults.json — "guid": "{0caa0dad-35be-5f56-a8ff-afceeeaa6101}"
    - Windows PowerShell (5.1): defaults.json — "guid": "{61c54bbd-c2c6-5271-96e7-009a87ff44bf}"
    - PowerShell 7/pwsh ("PowerShell", dynamically detected): PowershellCoreProfileGenerator.cpp
      — hardcoded constant PowershellCoreGuid = {574e775e-4f2a-5b96-ac1e-a2962a402336}, NOT
      computed via the generic dynamic-profile namespace+name hash (verified this does NOT
      match that formula's output — pwsh's profile is special-cased in Terminal's own source
      for backward compatibility, predating the generic hashing scheme WSL/SSH profiles use).
      Only applies if pwsh is actually installed and detected — a bare `"updates"` targeting a
      GUID with no matching profile silently does nothing, no error, so this is safe to ship
      unconditionally even on a machine without pwsh yet.

  font.face added 2026-08-17 (jetbrains-mono-nerd-font in packages.yaml) — needed for the
  Oh My Posh prompt's segment glyphs to render instead of tofu boxes, confirmed live on
  winlab.local. Updated 2026-08-19: now installed from the upstream ryanoasis/nerd-fonts
  release (see run_after_install-jetbrains-mono-nerd-font-windows.ps1.tmpl) instead of the
  DEVCOM WiX winget package — using the "...Mono" variant specifically, NOT the plain
  "JetBrainsMono Nerd Font" that dot_config/private_ghostty/common.conf.tmpl uses on
  macOS/Linux. Found live: the plain variant's `post` table has isFixedPitch=0, so Windows
  Terminal's font picker filters it out and silently falls back (survived a full reboot,
  ruling out a cache-timing issue) — the "...Mono" variant is properly flagged isFixedPitch=1
  and registers as "JetBrainsMono Nerd Font Mono" (its own nameID 16), which IS what Windows
  Terminal needs here even though it means the family name no longer matches Ghostty's exactly
  (same situation as the old DEVCOM build's Windows-only "JetBrainsMono NF").
*/ -}}
{{- /*
  Flat 12 for both profiles (changed 2026-08-20, user's explicit call) - the previous
  work=14/personal=13 split felt too large on Windows and had no equivalent on
  dot_config/private_ghostty/common.conf.tmpl (flat 13 there, no profile split at all) -
  inconsistent for no real reason. Bump this single number later if 12 turns out too small.
*/ -}}
{{- $fontSize := 12 -}}
{
    "profiles": [
        {
            "updates": "{0caa0dad-35be-5f56-a8ff-afceeeaa6101}",
            "font": {
                "face": "JetBrainsMono Nerd Font Mono",
                "size": {{ $fontSize }}
            }
        },
        {
            "updates": "{61c54bbd-c2c6-5271-96e7-009a87ff44bf}",
            "font": {
                "face": "JetBrainsMono Nerd Font Mono",
                "size": {{ $fontSize }}
            }
        },
        {
            "updates": "{574e775e-4f2a-5b96-ac1e-a2962a402336}",
            "font": {
                "face": "JetBrainsMono Nerd Font Mono",
                "size": {{ $fontSize }}
            }
        }
    ]
}
